UID:10143278-1794225600-1794229200@montclairlocal.news
SUMMARY:Lunch & Learn: Letting Go Without Losing Touch: Real answers for parenting tweens to young adults with Therapy Story
DESCRIPTION:Big emotions · Social media · AI · ADHD · The college launch \nA lunchtime meet-up for parents of middle and high school tweens and teens \nMonday\, November 9th\, 12:00–1:00pm Rec Room Montclair \nTherapy Story – Nayan Suryavanshi\, LSW and Kaci Mial\, M.Ed. \nGrowing up doesn’t happen on a schedule\, and neither does figuring out how to parent through it. You want to give them room to grow\, but between morning battles\, missing assignments\, and screen-time negotiations\, you’re stretched thin. \nMaybe… \n● You get “fine” when you ask about their day\, then hear them laughing on the phone with friends minutes later. \n● Every morning you’re the alarm clock\, the reminder app\, and the lost-and-found. By the time they’re out the door\, you’ve lost your cool and everyone’s running on empty. \n● You’ve reminded them about the same assignment five times\, found it unfinished at 11pm\, and wondered if it’s ADHD\, or if you have it too. \n● Their schedule is busier than yours\, with no time left. \n● Senior year is coming fast\, and you’re not sure whether they’re ready to let go\, or whether you are. \n● And somewhere in there\, you’re still trying to manage your own job\, life\, and sanity. \nIf any of this sounds familiar\, you’re not alone\, and this hour is for you. \nWhat to expect \nThis isn’t a lecture. It’s an open conversation with other parents\, guided by Nayan Suryavanshi\, LSW and Kaci Mial\, M.Ed. of Therapy Story and shaped by your questions. Anonymous question cards will be available. You’ll leave with a few go-to ways to get more than “fine\,” a clearer sense of when to worry\, and some new local parents in your corner. \nWhat we’ll talk about \n● Big emotions: Why they go from “fine” to slamming doors\, how to respond\, and when moodiness is a sign of something more. \n● Staying connected: What gets them talking\, staying calm when they push your buttons\, and repairing when you don’t. \n● ADHD: How it shows up in teens and adults\, and how it affects school\, work\, and family life. \n● School and social pressure: Friendships\, grades\, college apps\, or no motivation at all\, and how to protect their well-being through it. \n● Social media and AI: Screen time\, peer pressure\, and the AI chatbots teens are turning to. \n● Getting ready for what’s next: Building independence now so life after high school feels within reach. \n● Knowing when to get more support: When tools are enough\, when it’s time for more help\, and what teen or family therapy actually looks like. \nShare this:\n				Share on Facebook (Opens in new window)\n				Facebook\n			\n				Share on Bluesky (Opens in new window)\n				Bluesky\n			\n				Share on LinkedIn (Opens in new window)\n				LinkedIn\n			\n				Share on Threads (Opens in new window)\n				Threads\n			\n				Share on Nextdoor (Opens in new window)\n				Nextdoor\n			\n				Share on WhatsApp (Opens in new window)\n				WhatsApp\n			\n				Email a link to a friend (Opens in new window)\n				Email
URL:https://montclairlocal.news/event/lunch-learn-letting-go-without-losing-touch-real-answers-for-parenting-tweens-to-young-adults-with-therapy-story/
LOCATION:RecRoom\, 1 Lackawanna Plaza\, Ste. 6\, Montclair\, 07042\, United States
CATEGORIES:Parenting
